      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "samo",
          "text": "When Is the Lay-Down Method Used in Motorcycling?",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bamr",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "samq",
          "text": "Laying down a motorcycle refers to a rider deliberately tipping a motorcycle onto one side, typically in an attempt to avoid a more serious kind of motorcycle accident. Laying down a motorcycle is generally a last resort for a motorcyclist who believes he or she cannot stop the motorcycle in time to avoid a collision with a motor vehicle, a fixed object or an obstacle in the road.",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bamt",
      "style": "h2",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "sams",
          "text": "The Dangers of Laying Down a Motorcycle",
          "marks": []
        }
      ]
    },
    {
      "_type": "block",
      "_key": "bamv",
      "style": "normal",
      "markDefs": [],
      "children": [
        {
          "_type": "span",
          "_key": "samu",
          "text": "Laying down a motorcycle is generally viewed as less risky than a collision since it allows the motorcyclist to slide along the ground as opposed to crashing head-on. However, it is a widely misunderstood practice that can still cause serious physical injuries. Common types of injuries suffered include:",
